1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,960
So, Tom, this is the moment.

2
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:06,260
It's one of the great set piece moments, not just in French history, but in all European history, isn't it?

3
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:24,380
The humiliation of the king and queen by this group, either, as you would say, of tender hearted representatives of the people or slavering beasts from the slums and sewers, depending, of course, on the way you view it.

4
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:24,980
You frame it.

5
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:31,720
So the baker, Mrs. Baker, and the baker's boy, they're the king, the queen, and the dauphin, who's what, five, something like that?

6
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:32,820
Yeah, something like that.

7
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:34,140
Very young.

8
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:43,920
So they effectively, from this point onwards, if they weren't already, they are prisoners of the revolution, held in captivity.

9
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:49,960
And you could argue this is the great turning point in the story of the revolution?

10
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:54,660
I think certainly for the king and queen, it's a much more significant turning point.

11
00:00:54,660 --> 00:01:00,160
And it's clearly a massive turning point in the history of the revolution.

12
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:10,680
But you could equally argue that it is kind of drawing a line under a whole series of measures and developments that have been happening over the previous months.

13
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:13,620
It's a kind of logical conclusion to what's been happening.

14
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:18,020
So just to remind listeners of what those developments are.

15
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:24,380
The Bastille stormed on the 14th of July, and then that August of 1789.

16
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:34,740
And you have the revocation of feudal privileges, where all the aristocrats and abbots and people stand up and renounce all their various perks.

17
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,720
And that's passed on the 11th of August.

18
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:49,640
And then on the 26th of August, their approval is given in the National Assembly for the Declaration of the Rights of Man and the Citizen, which is what we were talking about in yesterday's episode.

19
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:54,280
But what we kind of briefly touched on, but didn't really explore, is the way that the...

20
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:56,920
The Declaration of the Rights of Man.

21
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:03,240
I mean, just on the ideological level, it raises as many questions as it answers.

22
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:10,360
And there are definitely those in the National Assembly, but also more broadly in Paris, who feel it does not go far enough.

23
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:21,080
So what we didn't talk about yesterday, which I'm sure lots of our particularly female listeners will have been wondering, is what about the rights of women and the citizeness?

24
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:24,640
And that is a topic that we'll be looking...

25
00:02:24,660 --> 00:02:25,020
We'll be looking at later.

26
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:33,800
Essentially, there is quite, I think, a kind of misogynist tone to revolutionary zeal.

27
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:38,480
Well, all the stuff about virtue and virility has been laying the ground for that, right?

28
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:43,840
They always talk about the oath of the Horatii, the great Romans, but they're always talking about men, never about women.

29
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:44,480
Yes.

30
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:50,660
And although women do play a key part in this story, they tend to be a kind of anonymous mass.

31
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:54,180
That is how they're celebrated, not as individuals.

32
00:02:54,980 --> 00:03:01,720
Also, the other kind of burning question, then as now, what about slaves?

33
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:05,880
The French own huge plantations in the Caribbean.

34
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:12,360
They say liberty for all, but equally they say property rights must be respected.

35
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:17,780
So there is an unresolved tension there, again, that we might explore in due course.

36
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:23,160
But of course, more immediately for the future of France and its constitution.

37
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:25,620
What about the monarchy?

38
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:32,180
Because how is the role of the king to be squared with the declaration and with the ideals of the revolution?

39
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:41,220
So yesterday we were looking at the church, but I mean, in a way, the king is an even bigger roadblock to that idea of a single nation, a single patrie.

40
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:54,400
Because as we were saying before, a lot of these revolutions are inspired by Montesquieu's ideas about a sort of a mixed constitution, a separation of powers.

41
00:03:54,660 --> 00:03:58,000
And the idea of the British constitutional monarchy as a model.

42
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:04,240
So something like the Comte de Mirabeau, who we've talked about, a great sort of figure in the assembly at this point.

43
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:07,500
He's all about a British style monarchy, isn't he?

44
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:09,180
And they need somebody to play that part.

45
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:12,380
What they're not talking about at this point is a republican democracy.

46
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:18,820
So they need, they want to have a king shaped whole and they want a king, a patriot king to fill it.

47
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:19,260
They do.

48
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:22,460
So Mirabeau is, you know, I mean, he's a hero of the revolution.

49
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:23,960
He's seen as a great patriot.

50
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:32,360
But he is allying himself with kind of nobles who are less motivated by revolutionary fervor.

51
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:34,980
So they, I mean, they are literally a metropolitan elite.

52
00:04:35,240 --> 00:04:42,920
These are nobles from Paris who are very rich, very highly educated, and who are fascinated by the British example.

53
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:53,360
So Anglomanie, the fascination with England as a model, has been a trend running throughout the 18th century, even as France has been at war with Britain.

54
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:54,420
And so.

55
00:04:55,100 --> 00:05:05,460
So Mirabeau and these kind of metropolitan nobles are proposing in the national assemblies, they've got a kind of constitutional committee set up to ponder these issues.

56
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:09,000
They're saying that we should model our constitution on British practice.

57
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:17,240
And although they are very, very careful to point out that they will, their version will be better than the British one, because they will learn from the British example.

58
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:21,060
No such version exists, even in theory.

59
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:23,240
You might think that.

60
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:24,520
So what they're proposing is.

61
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:37,760
An elected national assembly, which would correspond to the House of Commons, a Senate, which would be drawn from the great and the good, and obviously is corresponds to the House of Lords and this Senate initially they wanted to be for life.

62
00:05:37,940 --> 00:05:39,340
And then there are kind of objections to this.

63
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:42,640
And so they say, well, you know, it could, it could be for six years.

64
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:53,220
So drawing on the American exemplar, as you say, they want the king to play the part that the British king plays and essentially the British king.

65
00:05:54,520 --> 00:06:00,840
With the, the glorious revolution hanging over his or her head, the, the sense that a king who doesn't play his part can be kicked out.

66
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:07,840
The British king has the power of veto over all legislation, but essentially is very, very reticent in using it.

67
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:07,980
Yeah.

68
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:09,820
And I think that is what they want.

69
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:24,280
They want someone who will have the power of veto because that will, as you say, it will kind of provide a check on what they would see as excesses of, of popular fervor, but at the same time, they need a king who will not be.

70
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:25,500
Too ready to use it.

71
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:30,340
And the question is, is Louis the 16th qualified to play that role?

72
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:48,120
So that's one issue, but the other issue facing the monachia as they come to be called the monarchists is, um, the fact that there are lots of radical deputies in the assembly who view any prospect of the king having any kind of veto as an absolute outrage.

73
00:06:48,340 --> 00:06:48,560
Yeah.

74
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:54,180
And there are a lot aren't there who, one difference between the, the, the, as it were the British revolution, if you want to call it that.

75
00:06:54,520 --> 00:06:55,460
Which nobody ever does.

76
00:06:55,680 --> 00:07:06,180
And the French revolution is in Britain that the idea of the separation of powers as in the United States is seen as incredibly healthy and organic and, and, you know, necessary check and a balance.

77
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:14,060
But in France, you now have this sort of romantic fervor and the idea of la nation and the assembly reflects in the general will of the nation.

78
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:17,600
So there are lots of people who say, well, why would we divide it up?

79
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:20,220
And we've just done away with different orders.

80
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:20,600
Yeah.

81
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:24,020
So the three estates have all been swallowed up into the national assembly.

82
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:24,440
Exactly.

83
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:32,600
So why then, because essentially the, you know, if you're having a Senate of the great and the good, that means bishops, it means aristocrats.

84
00:07:32,740 --> 00:07:36,060
Um, so you, you can understand the, the suspicion.

85
00:07:36,680 --> 00:07:54,200
Um, so the Abbe Dessier who, who we met, um, in, uh, early on in the, the, the kind of the debate around the, the, um, the three estates Versailles, he describes the whole proposal as a letter de cachet launched against the national will against the

86
00:07:54,200 --> 00:07:55,040
the entire nation.

87
00:07:55,160 --> 00:07:55,480
Mm-hmm.

88
00:07:55,480 --> 00:08:00,620
So comparing it to a kind of unjust monarchical repression of liberty.

89
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:03,800
And that idea of the national will quite a dangerous idea.

90
00:08:03,920 --> 00:08:04,920
You might say in the long run.

91
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:13,500
Well, there also, but also the idea that sovereignty resides in, in the nation, which is, you know, one of the key principles of the, the declaration of rights.

92
00:08:13,720 --> 00:08:21,960
Um, and if that is the case, then how can any individual, even the king stand against the sovereignty of the nation?

93
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:24,020
And you know, it's a, it's.

94
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:28,640
It's a real problem, and it results in the National Assembly essentially kind of splitting

95
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:30,420
into rival factions.

96
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:38,240
And what happens is that the monaciens, the enthusiasts for the kind of British-style

97
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:43,300
constitution start to congregate on the right side of the meeting hall, and their opponents,

98
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:45,940
those who want a more radical solution, on the left.

99
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:53,040
And Dominic, that's a division that we still see stamped on politics.

100
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:53,160
Yeah.

101
00:08:53,300 --> 00:08:53,980
Left and right.

102
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:54,980
Absolutely.

103
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:56,080
To this very day.

104
00:08:56,180 --> 00:09:00,000
But actually, they reach a kind of compromise, don't they?

105
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:04,560
So this is, what, September 1789?

106
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:09,180
They reject a second chamber.

107
00:09:09,540 --> 00:09:14,780
That's decisively rejected, and the Constitutional Committee, which is full of all these kind

108
00:09:14,780 --> 00:09:17,560
of metropolitan elite types, that gets dissolved.

109
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:20,640
So that's clearly a victory for the more radical wing.

110
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:21,700
But you're right.

111
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:22,380
Around the veto.

112
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:26,280
There is a kind of compromise that doesn't really satisfy anyone.

113
00:09:26,660 --> 00:09:31,980
So the King is allowed a veto, but it's a suspensive one.

114
00:09:32,160 --> 00:09:37,540
So it's a bit like the House of Lords in, what was it, 1910 or something?

115
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:40,780
It's great to get Asquith back on the show, and Asquith's Britain.

116
00:09:42,260 --> 00:09:46,740
So yeah, the House of Lords can postpone legislation or sort of block it, but then basically the

117
00:09:46,740 --> 00:09:49,820
Commons can break the logjam under the Parliament Act.

118
00:09:50,020 --> 00:09:50,140
Yeah.

119
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:57,420
So the King can veto legislation three times, and then basically if they bring it back again-

120
00:09:57,420 --> 00:09:59,000
Which I think effectively means two years.

121
00:09:59,180 --> 00:09:59,540
Right.

122
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:02,560
So he has a two-year block on legislation.

123
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:02,600
Okay.

124
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:08,080
And then if they come back and say, no, we still want it, he then can't resist it.

125
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:12,700
Which actually, to me, seems quite a good, it's a safety valve, it's a cooling off period,

126
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:13,140
as it were.

127
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:16,880
But the right wing don't like it because it makes it impossible to preserve a kind of

128
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:17,860
genuine balance of powers.

129
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:18,100
Yeah.

130
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:19,340
So that's very important to them.

131
00:10:19,460 --> 00:10:23,160
And it doesn't satisfy the radicals because the King can still frustrate the will of the

132
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:23,740
sovereign people.

133
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:23,960
Yeah.

134
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:26,120
So both sides are kind of cross.

135
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:33,220
But there's also, I think, by this point, a sense that what people in the National Assembly

136
00:10:33,220 --> 00:10:40,280
think is not necessarily decisive because there is also the will of the people as expressed

137
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:43,060
by the inhabitants of Paris.

138
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:47,040
And in Paris, people don't understand the veto at all.

139
00:10:47,040 --> 00:10:49,780
They kind of think it's a new tax, lots of them.

140
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:53,260
And even those who don't, they don't really know what it is, but they're against it.

141
00:10:53,340 --> 00:10:54,400
That's the bottom line.

142
00:10:55,100 --> 00:11:00,280
And this is driven, I think, by, first of all, a kind of growing sense of hostility

143
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:01,860
to the King personally.

144
00:11:02,680 --> 00:11:08,220
And this is fostered by the fact that he has been notably silent about the repudiation

145
00:11:08,220 --> 00:11:10,400
of feudalism and the declaration of rights.

146
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:15,900
And I think we said in the previous episode, and then on the 19th of September, he does

147
00:11:15,900 --> 00:11:20,000
finally comment on this kind of repudiation of feudal rights, but he hedges it around

148
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:24,040
with so many qualifications that it ends up sounding like he's actually rejecting it.

149
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:26,060
And the deputies are very upset about this.

150
00:11:26,140 --> 00:11:28,540
And in Paris, everyone is absolutely furious.

151
00:11:29,240 --> 00:11:36,980
And then on the 4th of October, he voices reservations about the declaration of rights.

152
00:11:37,260 --> 00:11:44,080
And I suppose the King would say, well, why shouldn't I, I'm the King, but in the context

153
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:45,040
of a liberty loving.

154
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:48,380
Of course people with sovereignty, you know, this is dangerous.

155
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:57,060
And I think also there is a sense in Paris that the national assembly itself is compromised

156
00:11:57,540 --> 00:12:02,740
by its willingness to treat with the King and there's talk that they want to move it

157
00:12:02,740 --> 00:12:09,200
to from, from Versailles and the Paris region to tour the city on the law, which is famously

158
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:14,660
Royalist and would kind of therefore get them out of the crosshairs of, of the, of Parisian

159
00:12:14,660 --> 00:12:15,300
popular opinion.

160
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:17,000
And they're not in favor of that at all.

161
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:22,620
And the other thing that is happening in August and through into September, which everyone

162
00:12:22,620 --> 00:12:27,920
knows is incredibly problematic, uh, both for those who are starving and those who have

163
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:32,220
to keep a lid on, um, things that are bubbling away in France is the fact that the price

164
00:12:32,220 --> 00:12:33,760
of bread is starting to go up again.

165
00:12:33,980 --> 00:12:37,720
So it had declined kind of in early August, but it's now going up.

166
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:40,100
Um, and this isn't because there's terrible weather.

167
00:12:40,140 --> 00:12:40,860
The weather's very good.

168
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:43,200
The, the, the grain is ripening.

169
00:12:43,240 --> 00:12:43,800
It's kind of.

170
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:45,200
Ready to be harvested.

171
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:52,280
But the problem is that the, um, the weather is so good that all the mills have been immobilized

172
00:12:52,280 --> 00:12:53,540
because there's no water in the rivers.

173
00:12:53,680 --> 00:12:58,340
So you start to get bread riots, you get demands for higher wages, and you get these familiar

174
00:12:58,340 --> 00:13:02,860
accusations, which the Marquis de Sade makes such play with in his novels that the King

175
00:13:02,860 --> 00:13:06,900
and the aristocracy are deliberately trying to starve the revolution to death.

176
00:13:07,160 --> 00:13:09,200
So it's a useful reminder, isn't it?

177
00:13:09,240 --> 00:13:11,800
That there are these two different dimensions to the revolution.

178
00:13:11,900 --> 00:13:13,720
So on the one hand, there's the dimension of kind of.

179
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:18,000
Politics and ideas and everything that's going on in the national assembly as people

180
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,080
are moving into their different factions left and right.

181
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:24,980
And then outside, you know, none of that would have the salience and it wouldn't be so charged

182
00:13:24,980 --> 00:13:30,360
were it not for the fact that outside there are people starving, there is a, there is

183
00:13:30,360 --> 00:13:32,720
a real anger on the streets, isn't there?

184
00:13:32,780 --> 00:13:38,440
And the danger, the huge danger for the, the authors of the revolution is that they have,

185
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:41,980
you know, maybe on what could they do about it?

186
00:13:42,020 --> 00:13:42,460
But.

187
00:13:42,460 --> 00:13:50,340
They have allowed people, they've given people the impression that changes to the political

188
00:13:50,340 --> 00:13:58,060
and financial kind of structure will magically transform the situation on the streets and

189
00:13:58,060 --> 00:14:00,200
mean that bread prices fall and all that kind of thing.

190
00:14:00,700 --> 00:14:04,480
And actually a lot of them have known, you know, that they're, they're, they're as what

191
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:05,620
we would call neoliberals.

192
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:08,980
They have no intention of fixing the price of bread and, and helping people out and doing

193
00:14:08,980 --> 00:14:09,820
all these kinds of things.

194
00:14:10,060 --> 00:14:10,280
Yeah.

195
00:14:10,320 --> 00:14:10,520
Because.

196
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:12,880
Because May's radical could be September's conservative.

197
00:14:13,100 --> 00:14:13,360
Exactly.

198
00:14:13,540 --> 00:14:15,040
And things are moving so fast.

199
00:14:15,400 --> 00:14:21,320
So there's a sense that lots of deputies are kind of running very fast up a down escalator,

200
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:27,640
just trying to kind of avoid themselves being chewed up by, by, by, by the revolution.

201
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:33,220
And this is turbocharged by the fact, of course, that the declaration of rights has, has officially

202
00:14:33,220 --> 00:14:34,340
abolished censorship.

203
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:39,740
And that means that there's basically nothing the authorities can do to restrain.

204
00:14:39,740 --> 00:14:43,020
I mean, expressions of hostility, whether it's to the king or to the national assembly

205
00:14:43,020 --> 00:14:44,260
or, or, or, or whoever.

206
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:50,700
And there is a particular newspaper which is launched on the 12th of September.

207
00:14:50,900 --> 00:14:59,960
So against the backdrop of all these events, which really, really demonstrates how potent

208
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:02,020
journalism can now be.

209
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:07,080
And this is a paper, it's not its original name, but it's, it's, it's, it's ultimate

210
00:15:07,080 --> 00:15:07,860
name is .

211
00:15:07,860 --> 00:15:09,640
So the People's Friend.

212
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:18,960
And its editor is a man called Jean-Paul Marin, who is, he's basically, he's kind of drifted

213
00:15:18,960 --> 00:15:21,500
from job to job.

214
00:15:21,640 --> 00:15:25,480
He's someone who's never really held down a secure position.

215
00:15:25,740 --> 00:15:33,060
He's he's a scientist, he's a physician, he's a political theorist actually for a while,

216
00:15:33,160 --> 00:15:36,740
a bit like our gold hanger stablemate, Alan Shearer, Dominic.

217
00:15:36,760 --> 00:15:36,820
Yeah.

218
00:15:36,820 --> 00:15:38,200
