00:32:46,760 --> 00:32:48,040
Can I just interrupt at this point, Tom?

426
00:32:48,120 --> 00:32:51,620
So the interesting thing about them heading to Versailles is these women, the sort of

427
00:32:51,620 --> 00:32:52,500
fishwives, market women.

428
00:32:52,560 --> 00:32:53,400
They were known as poissard.

429
00:32:53,860 --> 00:32:56,620
And they would go to Versailles every year anyway.

430
00:32:56,800 --> 00:33:01,360
They used to go to Versailles on the 25th of August, the feast of Saint Louis, and they

431
00:33:01,360 --> 00:33:03,000
would give flowers to the queen.

432
00:33:03,420 --> 00:33:04,660
So it was a kind of ritual.

433
00:33:04,920 --> 00:33:08,460
And so she was used to seeing them and she actually, you know, you were saying about

434
00:33:08,460 --> 00:33:12,540
the thing with the and pretending, feeding the ducks and pretending, you know,

435
00:33:12,540 --> 00:33:14,360
to be an ordinary farm girl and stuff.

436
00:33:15,280 --> 00:33:21,980
They would do plays and stuff at the Versailles where they would affect the dialect of the

437
00:33:21,980 --> 00:33:22,540
fishwives.

438
00:33:22,540 --> 00:33:24,060
And they would use that.

439
00:33:24,120 --> 00:33:29,380
And these were very common in France, you know, the fishwives were, they were stock

440
00:33:29,380 --> 00:33:29,720
figures.

441
00:33:29,900 --> 00:33:31,240
They were laughing stock, I guess.

442
00:33:31,420 --> 00:33:36,640
And Marie Antoinette herself had kind of got people to try and teach her their lingo and

443
00:33:36,640 --> 00:33:36,880
stuff.

444
00:33:37,020 --> 00:33:39,620
So there is a dreadful irony at the back of all this.

445
00:33:40,360 --> 00:33:41,160
There is.

446
00:33:41,240 --> 00:33:46,780
And that is exactly the tension between Marie Antoinette's understanding of what a crowd

447
00:33:46,780 --> 00:33:51,500
should be and what a crowd might actually be.

448
00:33:51,680 --> 00:33:52,200
Yeah.

449
00:33:52,540 --> 00:33:57,160
And that's part of what makes it the whole experience that is coming so completely traumatic

450
00:33:57,160 --> 00:33:57,760
for her.

451
00:33:59,980 --> 00:34:03,740
And I don't think there's any ambivalence in the attitude of the women towards the queen.

452
00:34:03,860 --> 00:34:08,600
I think they are pretty universally hostile to her for all the reasons that we've been

453
00:34:08,600 --> 00:34:09,440
discussing.

454
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:13,420
Attitudes to Louis is a slightly more ambivalent.

455
00:34:13,580 --> 00:34:18,720
So they're calling him Le Bon Papa, the good father, and they want him to give them bread.

456
00:34:19,100 --> 00:34:21,860
And it's unclear whether they are.

457
00:34:21,880 --> 00:34:22,320
Oh, oh.

458
00:34:22,320 --> 00:34:28,480
They are calling him Le Bon Papa out of hostility, kind of sense of irony, or whether they genuinely

459
00:34:28,480 --> 00:34:28,980
mean it.

460
00:34:29,060 --> 00:34:34,180
And it may be that there's a kind of a gray area where they can't quite decide whether

461
00:34:34,180 --> 00:34:35,600
he is the enemy or their friend.

462
00:34:35,860 --> 00:34:41,960
But definitely they are also saying that if he is Le Bon Papa, he should be with his children

463
00:34:41,960 --> 00:34:44,420
in Paris, not stuck out in Versailles.

464
00:34:44,760 --> 00:34:50,100
And this increasingly becomes part of what they're talking about as they head out to

465
00:34:51,040 --> 00:34:51,840
the palace.

466
00:34:51,900 --> 00:34:52,300
Yeah.

467
00:34:52,300 --> 00:34:55,860
And as they go, they start picking up large numbers of men as well.

468
00:34:56,980 --> 00:35:00,860
And on their way, they meet with a contingent of the Flanders regiment.

469
00:35:03,340 --> 00:35:09,580
And they're nervous about this because the Flanders regiment is seen as the great defenders

470
00:35:09,580 --> 00:35:10,080
of the monarchy.

471
00:35:10,160 --> 00:35:12,320
But the Flanders regiment, they're all in favor of it.

472
00:35:12,380 --> 00:35:15,740
These are the people who have been accused of trampling the Cacade, but actually they

473
00:35:15,740 --> 00:35:17,340
turn out to be great enthusiasts for the revolution.

474
00:35:17,540 --> 00:35:20,400
And they greet the marchers and they say, brilliant, we're with you.

475
00:35:20,460 --> 00:35:20,800
Hooray.

476
00:35:20,800 --> 00:35:22,420
Let's get, let's crack onto Versailles.

477
00:35:22,920 --> 00:35:28,240
And this is an expression of a familiar problem that the king can't trust his own troops.

478
00:35:28,260 --> 00:35:29,360
Because troops are people too.

479
00:35:29,480 --> 00:35:29,740
Without troops.

480
00:35:29,820 --> 00:35:30,320
They're hungry.

481
00:35:30,500 --> 00:35:30,820
Yeah.

482
00:35:30,880 --> 00:35:32,160
They're impatient with authority.

483
00:35:32,280 --> 00:35:33,540
And they're like, yeah, why not?

484
00:35:33,620 --> 00:35:34,820
Actually, this is great.

485
00:35:34,860 --> 00:35:35,540
This is great fun.

486
00:35:35,660 --> 00:35:36,720
Let's all go to Versailles.

487
00:35:36,840 --> 00:35:41,360
You know, and troops have been disobeying their officers since the very beginning of the revolution

488
00:35:41,360 --> 00:35:41,960
for months.

489
00:35:42,280 --> 00:35:42,440
Yeah.

490
00:35:42,460 --> 00:35:47,560
And I think the fact that it is fun, you know, if you, if you have subject to kind of brutal

491
00:35:47,560 --> 00:35:50,620
discipline as dragoons are, or if you're, you know, you're starving.

492
00:35:50,620 --> 00:35:56,100
And hungry and you can't feed your children as so many of the women are quite aside from

493
00:35:56,100 --> 00:36:00,060
anything else, the chance to have a kind of expedition to Versailles, even if it's pissing

494
00:36:00,060 --> 00:36:04,280
down with rain is, I mean, it, it, it's, it's something exciting.

495
00:36:04,580 --> 00:36:08,220
It's so important in all revolutions and riots, actually, Tom, and something that I think

496
00:36:08,220 --> 00:36:14,320
historians sometimes underplay is, is exactly this point that one of the key things I always

497
00:36:14,320 --> 00:36:20,020
think in the momentum of any uprising, any rebellion, any riot is that the sense of the

498
00:36:20,440 --> 00:36:25,440
carnivalesque, I guess, of it being a tremendous laugh and actually, yeah, let's go for it.

499
00:36:25,480 --> 00:36:29,040
And the momentum and the giddiness of it all, and you really get that sense, don't you?

500
00:36:29,100 --> 00:36:33,100
The closer they get to Versailles, more and more people are joining them because this

501
00:36:33,100 --> 00:36:36,200
is a tremendous occasion and I can't, I don't want to, who knows what's going to happen.

502
00:36:36,280 --> 00:36:38,620
Let's, let's pile in kind of all this more women.

503
00:36:39,200 --> 00:36:45,540
There's a woman who looks amazing on a kind of, what's her name, who's the kind of Amazon.

504
00:36:45,620 --> 00:36:45,860
Yeah.

505
00:36:45,860 --> 00:36:49,560
So the, the Amazon, which is, and again, there's a kind of sense of cosplay there.

506
00:36:49,560 --> 00:36:52,800
I think that, you know, she, she's wearing a plumed hat.

507
00:36:53,080 --> 00:36:54,560
She's got a red riding coat.

508
00:36:54,780 --> 00:36:57,020
She's got loads of pistols in her belt.

509
00:36:57,160 --> 00:36:58,260
She's got swords.

510
00:36:59,020 --> 00:37:03,580
She's cuts a tremendous dash on her horse and, and she will always be kind of remembered

511
00:37:03,580 --> 00:37:08,860
as the, the kind of the, the emblematic figure of this march of the women, even though she

512
00:37:08,860 --> 00:37:10,360
is not a fishwife.

513
00:37:10,420 --> 00:37:12,520
I mean, she, you know, she's got a horse for starters.

514
00:37:13,560 --> 00:37:18,920
But the sense that there is a slightly carbon carnivalesque quality to what is happening.

515
00:37:19,120 --> 00:37:19,540
Yeah.

516
00:37:19,560 --> 00:37:24,020
And if it, I think that if it was just the, if it was just the women marching and even,

517
00:37:24,080 --> 00:37:29,420
you know, the, the members of the Flanders regiment, it wouldn't be quite as alarming

518
00:37:29,860 --> 00:37:34,740
as it turns out to be for the national assembly and for the king, because the other group

519
00:37:34,740 --> 00:37:39,580
of people who end up marching on Versailles is the, the national guard.

520
00:37:39,780 --> 00:37:45,460
So under Lafayette, who was supposed to be keeping order in Paris, and they are marching

521
00:37:45,460 --> 00:37:48,140
on Versailles five hours behind the market women.

522
00:37:48,260 --> 00:37:48,540
Yeah.

523
00:37:49,780 --> 00:37:51,180
What Lafayette's doing.

524
00:37:51,300 --> 00:37:56,120
He's basically appalled that his men want to go out and join the women on the march

525
00:37:56,120 --> 00:38:02,800
on Versailles, because he immediately understands that this is a altogether different quality

526
00:38:03,180 --> 00:38:09,520
of insurrection to a bunch of fishwives going out to Paris to have the, the, the officially

527
00:38:09,520 --> 00:38:13,300
mandated guard in Paris do that.

528
00:38:13,540 --> 00:38:19,060
I mean, that is, I know very, very kind of menacing revolutionary activity, an expression

529
00:38:19,060 --> 00:38:24,200
of hostility to the constitution and to the monarchy, which Lafayette is sufficiently a

530
00:38:24,200 --> 00:38:26,840
centrist dad, not to want to be involved in.

531
00:38:27,060 --> 00:38:30,540
Um, but it basically has, he has very little choice because I think he has the feeling

532
00:38:30,540 --> 00:38:34,760
that if he doesn't go along with his men, they leave the dessert or, or murder him or

533
00:38:34,760 --> 00:38:36,320
nothing good will come of it.

534
00:38:36,440 --> 00:38:42,480
So rather than morosely, he gets onto his horse and kind of rides out of their head.

535
00:38:42,520 --> 00:38:44,120
It's still driving rain.

536
00:38:44,420 --> 00:38:47,000
He's got 15,000 of the guard behind him.

537
00:38:47,040 --> 00:38:48,780
So that's an enormous number of people.

538
00:38:48,780 --> 00:38:55,340
And he heads off, uh, and one witness describes him as the prisoner of his own troops, which

539
00:38:55,340 --> 00:38:57,140
effectively is what he is.

540
00:38:57,320 --> 00:38:59,260
So you now have two groups of people.

541
00:38:59,340 --> 00:39:03,700
You have the, the, the, the women and you have the national guard, both descending on

542
00:39:03,700 --> 00:39:04,160
Paris.

543
00:39:04,900 --> 00:39:09,600
So the women arrive first, they try and break into, uh, the, the, the palace of Versailles

544
00:39:09,600 --> 00:39:12,420
itself, but the King's bodyguards and the Swiss guard turned them back.

545
00:39:12,600 --> 00:39:17,260
So then they march on the national assembly and they invade it and they demand to see

546
00:39:17,260 --> 00:39:17,580
the king.

547
00:39:17,580 --> 00:39:20,420
There are amazing descriptions of them in the national assembly by the way, because

548
00:39:20,420 --> 00:39:25,700
they're so, they're sodden and they're kind of, they're literally like steaming as they

549
00:39:25,700 --> 00:39:29,420
come in and they're, and they're, and you said the metropolitan elite kind of delegates,

550
00:39:29,640 --> 00:39:33,040
which basically they all are, they're all lawyers and stuff, aren't they, are sitting

551
00:39:33,040 --> 00:39:38,740
there kind of incredibly awkward and frightened, I think, and disturbed a lot of them as this

552
00:39:38,740 --> 00:39:43,820
great mob of people charge breaks into the assembly and people start like they start

553
00:39:43,820 --> 00:39:47,560
messing around with the debt deputies stuff and all that kind of chatter.

554
00:39:47,560 --> 00:39:48,160
Yeah.

555
00:39:48,320 --> 00:39:51,420
And they're kind of just, I mean, they're like a kind of walking pub, you know, walking

556
00:39:51,420 --> 00:39:55,980
holiday in the late district, they've all kind of burst in absolutely drenched and steaming.

557
00:39:56,320 --> 00:40:02,680
Um, so the deputies send a messenger to the king who's back now and they say, look, would

558
00:40:02,680 --> 00:40:04,260
you receive a delegation of the women?

559
00:40:04,360 --> 00:40:05,740
And he's, he says, yes, of course.

560
00:40:05,920 --> 00:40:10,260
So the women choose the perfect person to speak to him, which is a 17 year old flower

561
00:40:10,260 --> 00:40:14,820
girl called Pierrette Chabris, who is, um, she's very shy.

562
00:40:15,620 --> 00:40:16,900
Um, very pretty.

563
00:40:17,560 --> 00:40:20,540
Very virtuous looking, which is, you know, very important.

564
00:40:20,900 --> 00:40:22,320
She doesn't speak like a fishwife.

565
00:40:22,460 --> 00:40:25,480
She is renowned for her, her kind of genteel language.

566
00:40:25,780 --> 00:40:27,860
Um, so she has chosen the spokeswoman.

567
00:40:28,160 --> 00:40:29,260
She goes forward.

568
00:40:30,360 --> 00:40:34,980
She looks at the king and she's so overwhelmed that she faints and the king then fetches

569
00:40:34,980 --> 00:40:37,280
her smelling salts, um, helps her to her feet.

570
00:40:37,620 --> 00:40:38,940
It's all tremendous PR.

571
00:40:39,360 --> 00:40:44,980
Um, he then explains to the women that he, um, he's given orders for any grain that has

572
00:40:44,980 --> 00:40:46,720
been held up on the roads leading into Paris.

573
00:40:47,000 --> 00:40:47,480
Yeah.

574
00:40:47,560 --> 00:40:51,920
He delivered as a matter of absolute urgency that he'll do his best to source them more

575
00:40:51,920 --> 00:40:55,000
grain and the women are kind of mollified.

576
00:40:55,380 --> 00:41:00,140
But Louis knows even so that, you know, he is staring down a barrel here.

577
00:41:00,340 --> 00:41:06,340
And so that evening, unsurprisingly, he finally accepts both the abolition of feudalism and

578
00:41:06,340 --> 00:41:10,380
the declaration of rights, um, without any qualification whatsoever.

579
00:41:10,820 --> 00:41:16,640
And it is said that as he, as he, you know, he signs his approval, he does so with tears

580
00:41:16,640 --> 00:41:17,620
in his eyes.

581
00:41:17,720 --> 00:41:23,220
But Tom, this is just the first group because, uh, 15,000 men are also on their way.

582
00:41:23,460 --> 00:41:27,880
Um, and they're not really, I mean, Lafayette is now basically their trophy rather than

583
00:41:27,880 --> 00:41:28,580
their commander.

584
00:41:28,900 --> 00:41:34,860
Um, and, and they are a much more terrifying prospect than the market women are, aren't

585
00:41:34,860 --> 00:41:34,960
they?

586
00:41:35,060 --> 00:41:35,560
They are.

587
00:41:35,620 --> 00:41:39,520
And they are absolutely set on bringing the king and queen back.

588
00:41:39,740 --> 00:41:42,540
And when Lafayette arrives, he goes to the national assembly.

589
00:41:42,660 --> 00:41:44,700
He explains this to them.

590
00:41:45,100 --> 00:41:46,540
Um, and he then demands.

591
00:41:46,640 --> 00:41:51,420
An audience with the king who has retreated to the palace and the king sends a messenger

592
00:41:51,420 --> 00:41:54,560
out and says, yeah, you can come in, but you have to, you know, you have to be unarmed

593
00:41:55,100 --> 00:41:56,940
and Lafayette agrees to this.

594
00:41:57,340 --> 00:42:02,300
And so, um, a courtier comes that, you know, Chamberlain comes and gets him and he is led

595
00:42:02,300 --> 00:42:03,920
into the palace and dominate.

596
00:42:04,020 --> 00:42:09,520
You referenced this in the end of the first half, as he goes in a courtier sees Lafayette

597
00:42:09,520 --> 00:42:14,980
walking into the palace and says, there goes all of a Cromwell and Lafayette turns around

598
00:42:14,980 --> 00:42:16,460
and says, Cromwell.

599
00:42:16,640 --> 00:42:17,920
That would not have come unarmed.

600
00:42:18,060 --> 00:42:20,540
You may feel that that proves what you're saying.

601
00:42:20,780 --> 00:42:23,240
I think it's worth just stopping a second.

602
00:42:23,960 --> 00:42:25,860
Lafayette does have a tremendous opportunity here.

603
00:42:26,060 --> 00:42:27,980
He is a hero of the American war.

604
00:42:28,120 --> 00:42:31,060
He is arguably one of the two or three most famous men in France.

605
00:42:31,380 --> 00:42:37,480
He has under his command, you know, tens of thousands of men, potentially a different

606
00:42:37,480 --> 00:42:42,440
man, a Napoleon or a Cromwell could have turned that into a power base, a really ruthless,

607
00:42:42,620 --> 00:42:45,500
hard nosed political operator.

608
00:42:45,500 --> 00:42:47,380
I think it's to his credit that he doesn't.

609
00:42:47,400 --> 00:42:47,980
Of course you do.

610
00:42:48,120 --> 00:42:49,000
Of course you think that.

611
00:42:49,120 --> 00:42:49,760
But I mean, I don't.

612
00:42:49,780 --> 00:42:55,400
I think he's, I think, I think that he's, he's, um, he's a centrist dad and he is trying to

613
00:42:55,400 --> 00:43:00,640
ensure that the centre holds and he's doing his best, but we know that that is not going

614
00:43:00,640 --> 00:43:01,060
to succeed.

615
00:43:01,340 --> 00:43:06,420
But I think it's to his credit that he, he kind of, as it were, sticks to his guns, even

616
00:43:06,420 --> 00:43:09,000
though he will end up with not many guns by the end of the day.

617
00:43:09,040 --> 00:43:10,320
He doesn't stick to his guns.

618
00:43:10,360 --> 00:43:11,440
You know, there's other people take them.

619
00:43:11,480 --> 00:43:15,420
And anyway, listen, tell us what happens when he gets in to see the King.

620
00:43:15,500 --> 00:43:15,600
Okay.

621
00:43:15,660 --> 00:43:19,260
So he, like the women is, you know, he's drenched, he's spattered with mud.

622
00:43:19,380 --> 00:43:20,320
He's an absolute picture.

623
00:43:20,620 --> 00:43:28,220
Um, he comes into the, into, into Louie's presence and takes off his hat, sweeps all

624
00:43:28,220 --> 00:43:28,840
this kind of thing.

625
00:43:28,900 --> 00:43:33,980
And he, he, he, he declares in ringing tones, I have come to die at the feet of your majesty,

626
00:43:34,920 --> 00:43:39,920
obviously pre-prepared again, I imagine that this will confirm you in your sense of contempt

627
00:43:39,920 --> 00:43:40,380
for him.

628
00:43:40,440 --> 00:43:41,400
I like it.

629
00:43:41,480 --> 00:43:42,060
I like a man.

630
00:43:42,140 --> 00:43:43,840
He prepares a melodramatic statement.

631
00:43:43,840 --> 00:43:44,480
No, I mean, you've got to prepare.

632
00:43:45,500 --> 00:43:47,720
I think it's a good statement, but, but I, anyway, go on.

633
00:43:47,800 --> 00:43:48,120
Yeah.

634
00:43:48,200 --> 00:43:48,920
I think it's a good statement.

635
00:43:48,920 --> 00:43:49,060
Anyway.

636
00:43:49,100 --> 00:43:54,280
So, so, so, so he, so Lafayette then kind of reiterates to the King, look, you've got

637
00:43:54,280 --> 00:43:56,620
to get as much grain to the capital as you possibly can.

638
00:43:57,060 --> 00:44:01,440
And your majesty, I'm afraid that you have to come with, with me and the national guard

639
00:44:01,440 --> 00:44:02,100
back to Paris.

640
00:44:02,340 --> 00:44:05,800
You know, the national guard are not going to have it any other way.

641
00:44:06,200 --> 00:44:07,500
Louie agrees to the first request.

642
00:44:07,660 --> 00:44:10,880
I mean, he's, you know, he's already given orders that all the grain that can possibly

643
00:44:10,880 --> 00:44:15,100
be found, be sent to Paris as soon as possible, but he does stonewall the second.

644
00:44:15,100 --> 00:44:19,400
And he says, look, I'm going to have to consult with the queen and Mary Antoinette has already

645
00:44:19,400 --> 00:44:19,940
gone to bed.

646
00:44:20,240 --> 00:44:21,740
So we'll have to be left to the morning.

647
00:44:21,920 --> 00:44:25,600
So the King goes to bed about two o'clock Lafayette stays there.

648
00:44:25,680 --> 00:44:29,680
He wants to make sure that there's not going to be any trouble, but by about five o'clock

649
00:44:29,680 --> 00:44:30,400
he fails.

650
00:44:30,540 --> 00:44:30,860
Okay.

651
00:44:31,000 --> 00:44:31,920
So the night's passed.

652
00:44:32,060 --> 00:44:32,760
It's just before dawn.

653
00:44:32,880 --> 00:44:35,160
I could probably go and grab a few, a few winks.

654
00:44:36,080 --> 00:44:42,960
So he is married to a member of the Nye family, you remember, so he, they have a big house

655
00:44:42,960 --> 00:44:43,660
in Versailles.

656
00:44:43,760 --> 00:44:44,980
So he goes off there.

657
00:44:44,980 --> 00:44:48,140
And he finds a sofa and curls up and has a sleep.

